because I think whether it's dominant or not,

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:12.740
we all agree that these siloed test teams,

00:27:12.740 --> 00:27:13.700
these separate test teams,

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.540
are not a great way to make software,

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.580
but I think that's where selenium is being used a lot

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:21.380
and these teams, sometimes the only way for them to,

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:23.460
sometimes they don't even have an option

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:25.220
to communicate with the developers

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:25.860
and ask them to ask the ID tags.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.140
Right, which is just sad.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:27.860
That is very sad.

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:29.780
Yes, yeah, it breaks my heart.

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:30.420
It's sad.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:32.580
Well, the one I'm more worried about

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:37.540
is the one where there's a negative relationship

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:39.140
between the test and dev teams

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:43.460
and dev is not adding the device IDs

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.300
and we get feedback along those lines

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:49.890
and test says, okay, we'll screw you,

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:53.090
I'll go with CSS or the stylesheet approach

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.770
and work around the fact

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:55.970
that you're not helping us do the right thing.

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:57.250
Of course, it's a missed opportunity

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:58.290
to ask the question like,

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.530
should we even have tests at all?

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:01.730
Or at least at this level, right?

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:03.010
Yes, oh, yes.

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:06.610
I was setting you up for you to totally,

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:07.570
I was setting up the straw man.

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:11.730
You didn't take my bait.

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:14.850
Well, I was thinking about it earlier

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:16.530
so I'm going to ask you this question

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:18.530
and actually this is really good

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:21.250
because we can talk about the origins of selenium again

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:22.530
and these stories are all great.

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:24.690
You can just tell random stories about selenium

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.210
as much as you want and we'll fit them in

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:28.690
but the question I want to ask is

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.410
work with development teams using selenium

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:36.540
my hypothesis and my experience anecdotally

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:39.980
is that when dev teams own those selenium tests

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.980
they're actually very good about writing

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.260
only the tests they need

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:46.300
versus trying to automate everything with selenium

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.860
which unfortunately these we see more often

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:50.700
these silo test teams what they try and do.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.980
So how did, what are some heuristics you used

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:56.140
to figure out what you would test in selenium

00:28:56.140 --> 00:28:56.940
and what you wouldn't?

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:00.180
What I would test with selenium, what I wouldn't.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:01.860
I used to give a little talks about this

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:06.260
trying to mentally remind myself of all my little slideshows.

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:08.180
The number one thing would be

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:09.860
I think there's like failure modes, right?

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:11.860
That someone kind of gets super enthusiastic

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.020
and they just test anything that moves, right?

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:15.380
So I've got lots of opinions on this

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:16.740
but I can also kind of go back to

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:20.900
what we did originally like why it existed.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:22.100
Wait, wait, wait, Jason

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.740
why would we not test the things that don't move?

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:26.130
Whoa.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:26.610
Yeah, right.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:27.570
Okay, just broke my brain.

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:30.450
I'm going to ignore that question.

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:31.170
Yeah, fair enough.

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:33.490
So it started as a,

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:35.890
it really was from a regression point of view.

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:36.850
The very specific thing,

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:39.010
I call this like the sine wave of quality

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:42.450
where the reason why it was basically pencils down,

00:29:42.450 --> 00:29:44.690
screw this, we are going to solve this problem

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.130
and then selenium came out of it was

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:49.810
this dumb little button in Firefox is working

00:29:49.810 --> 00:29:52.610
and then someone did something to it,

00:29:52.610 --> 00:29:53.250
forgot what it was

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:55.010
but it would break it in Internet Explorer.

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:59.470
And so the bug report would be it's broken in IE, fix it.

00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:00.350
So they go and do that.

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:01.790
They push it in, we push it to production

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:05.230
of course, they've now broken it in Firefox, right?

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:06.830
So the next week we support,

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:09.150
we put in a bug saying Firefox is broken.

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:11.870
This little button in Firefox is broken, fix it.

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:13.550
So they go in and fix it.

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:14.590
When they test it in Firefox,

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:16.510
we push it to production and what happens?

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:17.630
It's now broken in IE.

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:18.430
So there was this like,

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:20.110
we were not making any progress

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:21.710
and we were regressing, right?

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:24.670
And so I kind of,

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:28.270
I forced this as a kind of like a no retreat,

00:30:28.270 --> 00:30:30.830
kind of like sometimes there's a different metaphor,

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:31.390
like a ratchet.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.070
Like you're kind of moving up and up

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:34.830
on like a roller coaster, right?

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:36.750
It keeps you from back sledding, right?

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:39.070
No, it sounds like you were on a merry-go-round.

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:39.550
What was that?

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:41.470
I said, it sounds like you were on a merry-go-round.

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:44.270
Or the caffeine is just finally kicking in, right?

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:44.770
Yeah.

00:30:46.670 --> 00:30:48.110
It was just, yeah, right.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:48.430
Oh, right.

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:49.550
The project felt whatever.

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:50.350
It was a circle.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:51.470
Time is a flat circle, right?

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:52.990
It was very frustrating.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:57.150
And so I kind of went with this observation, if you will,

00:30:57.150 --> 00:30:59.710
not an opinion just observing the team collectively

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:02.030
that you tell them to fix it in Firefox,

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:03.790
to fix it in Firefox because they're good developers,

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:05.630
but they're not covering every base.

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:08.430
And so we said like, let's automate this for one.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:11.300
And then two, like, let's solve it.

00:31:11.300 --> 00:31:13.300
So over time, over the first couple of months

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:16.100
of Selenium kind of being on the project,

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:18.420
we were finding little cases like that,

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:20.660
or we were kind of sort of backsliding.

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:21.540
Like we fixed one thing,

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:22.900
but we break something over there.

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:25.700
And so initially, basically the test names

00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:28.420
were kind of tracked to like whatever random regression

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:30.260
bug ID that they were linked to, right?

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:32.260
And I don't see this actually a lot in a lot of teams.

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:35.940
They kind of are more upfront, right?

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.060
So here's a feature.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:38.500
What's the automated test for that feature?

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:40.340
And there you go, right?

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:42.020
So over time, we eventually,

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:44.180
as new features were being created,

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:45.860
again, because the Selenium project came,

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:47.060
there were six months of development

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.220
before we had any user interface tool,

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:50.980
which of course is kind of potentially mind blowing

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.860
of like maybe that was a good thing.

00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:54.340
I don't know.

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:56.740
Like maybe all projects should go six months

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:58.340
and then do Selenium tests later.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:00.420
If they like maybe they're like,

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:02.020
maybe that's the prognosis.

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:03.300
Like don't do anything day one,

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.220
wait three months and then see if you need it, right?

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:05.780
I don't know.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:06.340
But the other thing,

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:09.540
eventually we added like new feature tests,

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:11.540
but we got a lot of value.

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:13.380
I got personally as like the project manager

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:15.860
of this thing, like, and whatever,

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:18.260
like I could finally see the team was not backsliding.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:20.100
And so kind of a regression testing point of view,

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:22.260
but also like in that context of like

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.860
cross browser regression testing,

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:25.140
making sure that when you,

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:26.900
we were specifically doing something on one browser,

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:28.340
we weren't inadvertently breaking something

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:29.700
on the other side of the room, right?

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:31.540
And I kind of, I get value in that.

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:33.220
But in that bigger context of what you're asking,

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:35.860
like, right, kind of we,

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:37.300
there was a million other kinds of ways

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:38.420
that we could do it, but, you know,

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:39.700
given that we had limited resources,

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:41.940
we kind of focused on those kinds of tests

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.620
that we thought were the most useful.

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:46.100
When I give kind of this little speech to other people,

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:46.900
I also kind of say like,

00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:48.980
you're going to write tests ahead of time,

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:51.300
or actually it doesn't matter the context,

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.140
but if you're going to like to start from zero,

00:32:53.140 --> 00:32:54.020
what would you test?

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:56.580
And one would be like the money path, I call it, right?

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:57.860
The, there's some path.

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:00.820
One, step one, whatever you're doing,

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:01.860
hopefully it's making money.

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:04.180
If it's not, solve that problem.

00:33:04.180 --> 00:33:05.700
Don't go write a bunch of selenium tests

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:06.980
if your startup is-

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:08.740
The more we automate, the more we'll make.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:10.500
Right, and so like A,

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:13.140
find money somehow, you know,

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:14.180
either profits or grants

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:16.020
or however you want to do it, right?

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.780
And then there is a money path, right?

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:19.300
So maybe don't spend so much time

00:33:20.260 --> 00:33:22.100
writing an entire framework around,

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:23.940
you know, changing the color settings

00:33:23.940 --> 00:33:26.020
or your header profile or your foot,

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:29.300
but there is a path where people can swipe a credit card

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:30.980
and give you money and sign up for the thing, right?

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:31.940
So if you're going to start somewhere,

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:33.300
focus on that

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:34.740
and then you kind of build things around it.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:35.620
Sometimes there's this metaphor,

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:37.860
it's not my metaphor, but very cliched.

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:40.820
Like it's kind of like paving the cow paths, right?

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:42.180
Where you kind of look where people are going

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:43.860
and then that's where you figure out

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:45.940
where to put the site, the concrete for the sidewalks.

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:48.420
I look at that, but it's also like following where,

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:50.100
where people,

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:51.700
like what's the most valuable path

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.700
through your application and start there.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:56.020
Eventually, maybe over time,

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:59.060
you go to the lesser used paths.

00:33:59.060 --> 00:34:00.900
But again, if it's kind of a focused developer team,

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:01.780
maybe they don't get to it.

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:04.180
You touched on something I want you to

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:08.100
set me straight on if needed, probably needed.

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:12.180
I haven't used Selenium in about three years,

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:14.420
but the last project I used it on

00:34:14.420 --> 00:34:17.780
was a project using Angular back at Microsoft.

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:22.980
And we had, and again, kudos to Jim Evans

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:24.900
for trying to make that IE WebDriver work.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:26.340
I think he's done some great work.

00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:28.100
I'm not sure where that was at the time,

00:34:28.100 --> 00:34:32.500
but we had a really hard time with our Selenium test

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:37.860
getting them to run across Chrome, IE, Firefox, and Safari.

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:40.660
Right. Yeah, that's unfortunate, right?

00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:42.420
Because it was started to be that, right?

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:45.300
So it's like, it's not living up to its raison d'etre.

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:47.460
If that was like in that moment,

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:49.220
like it needed to be a cross browser tool.

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:51.420
Yeah, because that's where we see,

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:54.060
like the story used to describe,

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:55.820
like if you want to make sure these elements

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:59.580
continue to work across browsers, super powerful.

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:01.900
But there is a parallel universe here.

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:03.340
Like, so there's from like a, you know,

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:05.740
correct way, like if I'm meditating on that phrase

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:07.980
of like they solve the wrong problem really well, right?

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:10.940
One way that we could have solved that problem well,

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:12.620
in a completely different way,

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:17.660
is John Resig created the jQuery project, right?

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:22.300
Same goal was JavaScript is super buggy cross browser.

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:24.140
And so it created one API

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:26.700
that if you write to the jQuery API,

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:28.780
then you've solved your cross browser problems.

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:31.020
Very elegant solution to the same problem,

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:32.860
completely different context, right?

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:35.820
So like there's a different kind of recommendation there,

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:37.340
right, is not so much use jQuery,

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:39.260
but there's a certain level like find ways

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.620
to avoid the cross browser testing problem

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:45.260
without necessarily throwing all the weight

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:47.740
at the selenium level or the testing level, right?

00:35:47.740 --> 00:35:49.500
Maybe there's a different framework you can use

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.650
or just give up, quit, go hiking across the country

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:54.290
and give up all the way.

00:35:54.290 --> 00:35:59.360
No, I mean, I'm actually thinking, so there's,

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:03.040
I see a move, I don't know if this is going to take off,

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:09.760
but I see a lot of browser companies basing on Chrome now,

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:16.900
and I wonder if essentially that sort of standardization

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:18.580
is going to keep pushing forward

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.980
to what degree that will eliminate cross browser.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:27.460
Yeah, I sadly am, I don't want to speak for you guys,

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:30.290
but like there's a certain,

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:34.210
I guess I'm now at risk of being that guy

00:36:34.210 --> 00:36:36.210
who's seen this, the business cycles,

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:37.730
and now I'm in the corner saying,

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:39.650
we've done this all before, like,

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:42.700
basically like note one, no one listens to that guy,

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:45.980
but the person, that guy or girl is also totally right,

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:47.980
right, like where everything is a fad,

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:49.900
everything's been done before.

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:50.940
There's a certain level of like,

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:53.340
so if nothing else, like to your point,

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.100
like there's kind of browser consolidation,

00:36:55.100 --> 00:36:56.300
everyone's kind of like choose,

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:58.140
like getting on the bandwagon,

00:36:58.140 --> 00:36:59.500
it's all going to be Chrome.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:01.020
I would look at it saying like, well,

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:04.540
inevitably Google will become like Microsoft

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:06.940
and realize it has all the power of the universe

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:08.380
and go super evil,

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:11.980
and then naturally some Firefox-like thing

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:16.060
will come out of that and punch the air,

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:17.020
like shake their fists,

00:37:17.020 --> 00:37:18.540
kind of like give a Braveheart speech

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:19.420
and say Google is evil,

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:20.780
we're going to do this other thing,

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:22.220
and it will effectively,

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:25.180
there'll be another 10 years, 15 years of the Firefox,

00:37:25.180 --> 00:37:26.540
there'll be a new Firefox

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:28.780
that will fight the old evil Chrome.

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:29.820
This is like inevitable, right?

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:32.380
So I'm not too worried about Chrome,

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:33.980
like a kind of a monoculture around Chrome

00:37:33.980 --> 00:37:36.540
because if it gets too much of a problem,

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:39.090
it will then, history will repeat itself

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:42.130
and then there will be kind of a blossoming

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:43.170
of a bunch of options.

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:45.010
But right now we're in kind of a consolidation mode

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:47.650
and that's just, I don't know,

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:48.770
I guess I just look at it,

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:50.290
it's like, I guess it's maybe a problem,

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:51.890
but I'm not so fired up about it.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:52.930
Got you.

00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:55.170
Magically, somehow it'll solve itself in 10 years,

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:56.130
I guess is what I'm trying to say.

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:58.290
In hindsight, I'm wondering,

00:37:58.290 --> 00:37:59.490
I have two thoughts in my head,

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:01.010
I'll see if I can keep them straight.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.690
But one thing I'm wondering is

00:38:03.890 --> 00:38:06.450
if those Selenium tests we had were just,

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:08.690
I mean, they were fairly simple,

00:38:08.690 --> 00:38:09.810
but maybe too complex.

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:11.250
Maybe what you want to test there is

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:14.210
for cross browser compatibility,

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:17.010
I'm wondering if there is some heuristic around

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:18.130
writing simple tests

00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:19.410
and the simpler the test is

00:38:19.410 --> 00:38:21.570
or the less crazy you try and get,

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:24.660
maybe the more apt that test is to run,

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:25.780
successfully across browsers.

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:27.540
Yeah, it sounds like we could

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.020
put a consulting hat on there,

00:38:30.020 --> 00:38:30.980
I'd love to dive down

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:32.500
and specifically see the lines of code

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:35.060
and see why is it working here and not there.

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:38.020
And to be fair, it's been three years

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:42.420
and there's so much progress on all fronts

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:44.020
on the WebDriver projects that it's...

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:46.980
No, thanks to me, absolutely not.

00:38:46.980 --> 00:38:49.780
Like this is all thanks to Jim and Simon

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:51.060
and there's a million other people

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:53.060
that are day to day in the trenches

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:54.020
working on the project now.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.020
The other thought in my head is

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:57.700
because of the Braveheart quote,

00:38:57.700 --> 00:38:58.740
now I'm wondering if that's where

00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:00.580
the Brave browser got their name from.

00:39:01.790 --> 00:39:02.270
I don't know.

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:04.270
Which Braveheart quote?

00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:06.750
Oh, I was just imagining,

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:08.750
in my mind, I was just imagining,

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:11.070
was it, I'm blanking out his name,

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:13.470
but it's the actor,

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:15.710
Mel Gibson, like just standing on his horse,

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:17.390
just encouraging people to go into battle.

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:19.470
I can't even remember why I was saying.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:21.390
Anyway, it was the rallying cry.

00:39:21.390 --> 00:39:22.430
Yeah, right, right, right.

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:24.030
Yeah, someone's going to have some rallying cries

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:26.190
saying we need to fight these browsers.

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:27.070
Whatever.

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:27.630
Where were we?

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:29.550
You're asking two old guys

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:32.190
who can't remember where they park their cars.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:32.990
I don't know.

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:34.430
Well, so the one thing

00:39:34.430 --> 00:39:36.350
that Jason started talking about

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:37.230
reminded me of...

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:38.270
I can just sense so many people

00:39:38.270 --> 00:39:39.550
rolling their eyes listening to this right now.

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:41.950
We expected a fight.

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:44.110
No, the...

00:39:44.110 --> 00:39:44.910
I am the steel...

00:39:44.910 --> 00:39:45.950
I am the top Google search

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:47.230
for steel cage knife fight, by the way.

00:39:47.230 --> 00:39:48.430
I want to point that out.

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:49.150
You are? Why?

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:51.550
Well, it's a funny story.

00:39:51.550 --> 00:39:53.230
So everyone, stop what you're doing,

00:39:53.230 --> 00:39:55.070
Google the phrase steel cage knife fight.

00:39:55.630 --> 00:39:57.310
And the story there was...

00:39:57.310 --> 00:39:58.830
This was the Google test automate...

00:39:58.830 --> 00:39:59.790
I totally cut you off, Brent.

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:00.030
I'm sorry.

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:00.750
No, no, it's fine.

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:01.310
Story time.

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:04.750
This was the Google test automation conference 2007.

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:08.430
So this is Google's second test conference.

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:10.110
And there's a whole awesome backstory

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:11.790
on even why Google did this test conference

00:40:11.790 --> 00:40:12.590
for a couple of years.

00:40:12.590 --> 00:40:14.030
And I've got some conspiracy theories

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:14.910
on why they stopped doing it.

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:16.910
But anyway, not important to the story.

00:40:16.910 --> 00:40:19.390
So I'm there in New York City

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:23.950
and I was drafted in to give a talk to replace a speaker.

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:25.230
At that time, I was a Googler.

00:40:25.230 --> 00:40:27.070
I'd been there for six months.

00:40:27.070 --> 00:40:29.470
Simon Stewart was still at ThoughtWorks

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:31.550
and I had just left ThoughtWorks the year before.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:34.190
He was there to present WebDriver.

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:37.630
And I had seen WebDriver

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:39.550
when we were both at ThoughtWorks a year earlier.

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:45.980
And anyways, I was ready to not be impressed by his talk.

00:40:46.540 --> 00:40:49.420
And also, there were a lot of Selenium talks at GTAC.

00:40:49.420 --> 00:40:52.620
And also, I was super awkward and weird about it, right?

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:55.500
Because I didn't realize it was being used that much.

00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:59.500
Anyway, so I was sitting through Simon's talk

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.580
and I realized he had fixed...

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:03.340
I'm kind of sitting there with crossed arms thinking like,

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:06.800
OK, waiting to take notes and shoot everything down.

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:08.640
And he had fixed all of the problem.

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:11.440
Every conceptual, architectural, whatever you want to call it,

00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:14.160
idea that I was against WebDriver and how he did things.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:15.360
He fixed all those problems

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:16.640
and it was awesome and brilliant.

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:20.880
In an instant, as I'm watching his talk about WebDriver,

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:21.760
I realized this is it.

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.160
This really is the Selenium killer.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:26.720
This is all the things I would wish we could do

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:28.160
in a future version of Selenium.

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:29.840
So then I had kind of a choice there.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:31.840
It could either be my new best friend

00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:34.240
or I could find some weird passive-aggressive way

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:35.440
to super hate on him, right?

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:37.680
Because he's clearly made the better tech.

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:39.840
So these were our presentations, right?

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:41.840
The other footnote to history, my talk was

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:45.520
I created an AWS account two days before

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:48.320
and got access to like 20 machines in the cloud.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:51.760
And I wondered if I could run a browser test in the cloud.

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:52.560
I thought that would be interesting.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:53.840
And this is like 2007, right?

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:56.160
So that was actually AWS had only been around

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:57.680
for maybe six months or a year at that point.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:41:58.960
So this was like a new thing in the world.

00:41:58.960 --> 00:42:00.720
Anyway, side note to that thing.

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:03.200
But the reason why Steelcage and I fight,

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:04.080
long story long,

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:06.000
they were lightning talks at the very last day.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:09.040
And so I proposed kind of as a way to encourage this.

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:12.000
He's either figuring out whether he's my new best friend

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:13.280
or my new enemy.

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:16.240
I proposed doing a dueling lightning talk

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:20.080
where we would both talk about Selenium and WebDriver

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:22.800
as a kind of set it up as a debate.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:24.160
Kind of like in Wink and Douglass debate

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:25.200
or something like that, whatever.

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:28.160
But with the caveat where I would have to argue

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:31.840
for and against WebDriver and he would have to argue

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:34.560
or I would have to argue for like five reasons

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:36.400
why WebDriver is awesome and Selenium is horrible.

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:37.920
That would be my side.

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:39.360
And then Simon would stand up there

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:41.520
and give five reasons why WebDriver is horrible

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:42.400
and Selenium is awesome, right?

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.920
So we would like flip sides, arguably, whatever.

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:49.600
Anyway, so the thing was that conversation

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:52.480
and I can't believe he went along with it

00:42:52.480 --> 00:42:52.960
and we did this thing.

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:54.640
But there was like a lot of ha ha,

00:42:54.640 --> 00:42:56.880
I'm serious kind of conversations of like,

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:58.720
well, what if we merged projects, right?

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:00.080
What if we joined forces?

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:02.880
And so that it kind of started at that,

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:06.240
you know, GTAC 2007 New York City.

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:08.160
That's where the WebDriver,

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:10.320
like the idea of WebDriver joining this Selenium project

00:43:10.880 --> 00:43:11.600
started.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:13.040
And it started with kind of like, yeah,

00:43:13.040 --> 00:43:16.640
this very kind of joke and idea of like,

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.240
let's do a steel cage knife bite where of course

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:20.000
it comes out that we're just all friends, right?

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:21.280
No, I think that was brilliant.

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:24.400
Particularly, you know, if you felt beforehand

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:26.080
that WebDriver was the better product,

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:27.120
which is what I heard, right?

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.760
You're like, let's set up the game so I win.

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:30.260
Well done.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:34.720
Well, there was a specific idea that he implemented

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:38.240
that I kind of like, oh, that was the wrong idea.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:40.480
There was something in the water,

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:43.600
like with there's an older framework idea

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:45.280
called FIT, Framework for Integrated Testing.

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:46.960
Ward Cunningham, more famous for creating,

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:50.240
right, right, more famous for creating Wikipedia,

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:51.840
or sorry, not Wikipedia, but the Wiki.

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:56.080
But then there was other projects that are out there

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:57.200
where they're kind of running with this idea.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:58.560
And even with the first version of Selenium,

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:01.040
also kind of ran with the idea for implementing FIT.

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.760
My first, my one and only maybe email to Ward Cunningham

00:44:03.760 --> 00:44:05.760
was like, has anyone done FIT for JavaScript?

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:07.200
If not, great, I'm going to go do that, right?

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:09.200
So that was like part of its tagline.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:10.880
And I realized at some point later, like, wow,

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:14.640
that was like a bad idea to do that.

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:16.400
Especially if it's a developer tool,

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:18.720
like if you're writing a developer tool for developers,

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:20.640
they would rather write in Python or Java

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:21.920
or C Sharp or whatever.

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:23.600
They don't want to write in HTML tables

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:24.240
or something like that, right?

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:24.960
It was kind of ridiculous.

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:28.000
But there are other frameworks that are still out there

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:30.000
that still use that.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:33.680
And when I first saw Simon's first implementation

00:44:33.680 --> 00:44:35.680
of WebDriver, he was also kind of doing

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:39.120
that first kind of user interface or facade into it

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:41.520
was like a fit like we had to write your tests and tables

00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:43.120
and we're like, ah, man, here we go again.

00:44:43.760 --> 00:44:46.160
But when he gave that presentation in New York,

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:47.040
he got rid of all of that.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:48.640
He just got to like the chocolatey goodness

00:44:49.280 --> 00:44:52.640
of the stuff and the like just footnote to history.

00:44:52.640 --> 00:44:54.880
But the specific thing was Selenium 1,

00:44:54.880 --> 00:44:56.640
like at that time, the state of the art for Selenium,

00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:58.720
everything was emulated in JavaScript.

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:00.880
Again, same way that Cypress is implemented now.

00:45:00.880 --> 00:45:03.680
It's all injected JavaScript in the browser.

00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:06.000
And Simon solved it kind of at the operating system level

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:07.920
where you would ask the operating system

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.000
to click a button or something like that.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:13.520
So as far as the browser is from the browser's perspective,

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:17.040
the events were more native and less flaky

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:18.080
or something like that.

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:20.160
Anyway, I'm getting super in the weeds here.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:22.000
But where were we?

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:24.560
One thing I wanted to mention

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:27.200
and then I'll ask for probably some closing comments

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:29.280
from you or anyone you can get to.

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:31.520
I went to maybe the last,

00:45:31.520 --> 00:45:34.880
I think it was the next to the last GTAC conference.

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:35.520
Yeah.

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:39.680
And ironically, I may actually be using that word

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:40.960
correctly in this case.

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:44.800
Or yeah, I nicknamed it the flaky test conference

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:47.920
because every single talk had some flavor

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:50.480
of talking about how to deal with flaky tests.

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:51.280
Right.

00:45:51.280 --> 00:45:52.800
And I think it's just interesting

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:56.080
that came up in my head while you were talking.

00:45:56.320 --> 00:45:57.840
I don't know why you need to,

00:45:57.840 --> 00:45:59.840
I mean, it's one slide.

00:45:59.840 --> 00:46:01.680
It just says the word delete.

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:03.680
Right.

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:05.600
What I find fascinating was why Google

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:09.360
started that series of conferences.

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:11.760
The first one was LTAC.

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:13.840
So it was the London Test Automation Conference.

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:16.480
Alan Hutchison based in the New York,

00:46:16.480 --> 00:46:18.480
or sorry, in the London office there.

00:46:20.370 --> 00:46:22.530
Here's the thing about the whole GTAC conference.

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:23.730
Secretly it was,

00:46:23.970 --> 00:46:25.970
oh, what they were finding was that

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:28.130
it's really hard to find developers

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:29.650
who are interested in testing.

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:31.810
So this was a way of flushing people out in the world

00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:35.090
of the self-identify as people

00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:36.610
who are just totally geek out on this stuff.

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:37.010
Right.

00:46:37.010 --> 00:46:40.780
It's really hard to find that developer,

00:46:42.060 --> 00:46:43.340
test-minded developer, if you will.

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:45.900
So for a couple of years, it was secretly,

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:49.420
secretly, very secretly on the lowdown,

00:46:49.420 --> 00:46:51.740
kind of a recruiting effort for Google.

00:46:51.740 --> 00:46:52.140
Right.

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:53.660
At some point, the cat was out of the bag

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:55.260
and it became obviously a recruiting thing.

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:57.660
So why would people then send their employees

00:46:57.660 --> 00:46:59.340
off to a Google conference

00:46:59.340 --> 00:47:01.260
to then just be poached and then leave?

00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:01.500
Right.

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:05.020
So once that was obvious, it was impossible.

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:06.060
Who's going to go?

00:47:06.060 --> 00:47:07.660
If you're not already a Googler,

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:08.940
why would you send your employees there?

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:09.260
Right.

00:47:09.260 --> 00:47:10.620
But there was a good couple of,

00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:12.700
there was some beautiful years there at the beginning

00:47:12.700 --> 00:47:14.860
where there really wasn't a developer

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:17.900
really geeky, really nerdy in the weeds.

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:20.060
Let's just tons and tons of code,

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:22.460
developer-focused testing conference.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:24.540
The other kind of context here was like,

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:25.980
I didn't even know when we started Selenium

00:47:25.980 --> 00:47:27.500
that testing was a profession,

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:28.140
that there were books,

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:29.500
that there were conferences

00:47:29.500 --> 00:47:30.700
and all kinds of things like that.

00:47:30.700 --> 00:47:33.020
So kind of leading like in forms,

00:47:33.020 --> 00:47:34.540
and I would only go to developer conferences

00:47:34.540 --> 00:47:37.580
like JSConf or PyCon or things like that.

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:38.540
Only after a couple of years,

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:41.260
I finally went to see testing conferences

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:43.340
like Star East, Star West,

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:45.500
or SDPCon, things like that.

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:48.060
But Alan was specifically trying to find developer,

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:50.860
test-minded developers, if you will.

00:47:51.180 --> 00:47:53.660
And he felt like there wasn't a lot of real

00:47:53.660 --> 00:47:55.820
in the weed, gnarly geekiness.

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:57.100
So to fix stuff like you asked,

00:47:57.100 --> 00:47:57.660
yes, you could delete it.

00:47:57.660 --> 00:48:00.220
I think in 2007, I completely agree with that.

00:48:00.220 --> 00:48:02.220
I think, I don't think that's...

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:04.540
Back in 2007, like 12 years ago,

00:48:05.260 --> 00:48:07.180
yeah, I completely agree with that.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:13.020
The separation of roles was a big thing

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:13.900
at a lot of companies.

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:15.900
Microsoft still had a test team, right?

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:16.540
For sure.

00:48:16.540 --> 00:48:19.580
They had almost 10,000 testers at that time.

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:22.770
So finding test-minded developers.

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:25.890
Matter of fact, I think 2007 is even

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:28.610
sort of pre the dominance of TDD

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:32.050
and sort of design constructs

00:48:32.050 --> 00:48:35.250
that now are very popular in the industry.

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:39.630
So I don't think that world exists today.

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:42.590
I wonder how often or to what degree I'm right.

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:45.390
I'm thinking, again, putting myself

00:48:45.390 --> 00:48:47.870
in the seat of the listener here,

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:48.990
and I'm just thinking like listening

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:50.190
to myself tell all these wonderful,

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:51.950
cool stories, which I thought was a good idea.

00:48:51.950 --> 00:48:52.510
Maybe it is.

00:48:52.510 --> 00:48:52.910
I don't know.

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:53.310
Probably not.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:54.110
But I'm thinking like, okay,

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:56.750
was there any actionable thing going forward?

00:48:56.750 --> 00:48:58.590
Like, okay, now we know what the industry

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:00.110
was like a couple of years ago.

00:49:00.110 --> 00:49:01.710
Like, yeah, now what?

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:02.110
So what?

00:49:02.110 --> 00:49:03.310
Okay, go eat croissants.

00:49:03.310 --> 00:49:05.550
That's the moral of the story.

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:10.910
So actually, yeah, so it's time for us to close,

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:11.790
but why don't you...

00:49:13.070 --> 00:49:14.270
Here's a suggestion.

00:49:14.270 --> 00:49:18.930
Jason, do you have any regrets

00:49:19.010 --> 00:49:21.650
around how Selenium is being used today,

00:49:21.650 --> 00:49:23.970
or do you have any guidance around how...

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:28.210
So again, I mentioned, I think I can make an argument

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:31.170
it's immoral, or it's UI automation

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:32.450
to some degree is immoral.

00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:35.890
And yes, that's hyperbole,

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:38.290
but I think all three of us know

00:49:38.290 --> 00:49:42.050
that that is non-tribially true

00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:44.290
when we look at it from a business context.

00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:47.090
Anything you want to mention on that regrets,

00:49:47.090 --> 00:49:49.250
like, is there one thing,

00:49:49.250 --> 00:49:51.170
if you had an audience to tell testers,

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:55.250
hey, please stop doing this with my baby,

00:49:55.250 --> 00:49:56.130
what would you want to say?

00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:57.730
Oh, man.

00:49:58.850 --> 00:49:59.810
And that could be anything.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:02.290
It doesn't have to be even related

00:50:02.290 --> 00:50:03.570
to what we talked about today.

00:50:04.300 --> 00:50:05.420
I guess I would just say,

00:50:05.420 --> 00:50:07.180
web testing is just so yesterday,

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:08.620
it's all about robots right now.

00:50:12.620 --> 00:50:13.100
Stop.

00:50:13.820 --> 00:50:16.300
Really, I've got a lot to say on that stuff,

00:50:16.700 --> 00:50:18.220
I don't know how to put a bow on that.

00:50:18.220 --> 00:50:19.340
There's a certain level of like,

00:50:20.140 --> 00:50:22.060
I'm that dangerous engineer who,

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.900
that whole like, they spent so much time

00:50:23.900 --> 00:50:24.940
figuring out how they could do it,

00:50:24.940 --> 00:50:27.420
they don't spend enough time deciding,

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:28.620
figuring out, spending enough time

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:29.660
thinking about whether they should.

00:50:30.620 --> 00:50:32.620
I put myself in this role where it's like,

00:50:34.460 --> 00:50:36.700
where I look, I get motivated

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:39.500
when someone tells me something is impossible.

00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:40.940
Selenium came from a time

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:43.180
where people said it is impossible

00:50:43.180 --> 00:50:44.620
to test JavaScript, right?

00:50:44.620 --> 00:50:45.500
And I rejected that.

00:50:45.500 --> 00:50:46.620
And so Selenium came from like,

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:48.300
the massive motivation was like,

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:49.260
ta-da, here it is.

00:50:49.260 --> 00:50:50.700
Here is a tool that you can use

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:51.740
to test JavaScript, right?

00:50:52.540 --> 00:50:54.220
I wanted to win that argument.

00:50:55.100 --> 00:50:56.540
It's a completely different argument,

00:50:56.540 --> 00:50:58.460
but it needs to happen on whether you should,

00:50:59.330 --> 00:51:00.610
I want to kind of take off the table,

00:51:00.610 --> 00:51:02.530
you shouldn't do it because you can't do it.

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:05.490
I feel like that is not a great argument.

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:08.770
It can shut down conversations for sure,

00:51:08.770 --> 00:51:10.130
but I don't like that.

00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:12.290
I would rather encourage people to say,

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:13.410
assuming we can do it,

00:51:13.410 --> 00:51:14.770
and assuming we can totally do it well,

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:16.050
we can solve that problem really well.

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:18.290
Should we be solving that problem, right?

00:51:18.290 --> 00:51:21.250
And this is where we totally agree.

00:51:21.250 --> 00:51:24.770
Find that higher level above the browsers.

00:51:25.490 --> 00:51:26.930
Should we be writing a web app?

00:51:26.930 --> 00:51:27.890
Should it be a mobile app?

00:51:27.890 --> 00:51:29.970
Should we be making an app at all, right?

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:32.290
Try to find that.

00:51:32.290 --> 00:51:33.490
And maybe some of these decisions

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:34.690
kind of come out of it.

00:51:34.690 --> 00:51:36.450
**Jade,** So you mentioned robots.

00:51:37.730 --> 00:51:39.410
One of our listeners asked a question

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:40.610
on Slack the other day,

00:51:40.610 --> 00:51:42.050
and one of the opinion,

00:51:42.610 --> 00:51:44.210
hey, specifically,

00:51:44.210 --> 00:51:48.210
do we think AI is going to kill Selenium?

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:55.330
Now, I essentially said a very long worded version of

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:57.650
not anytime soon.

00:51:59.090 --> 00:51:59.890
But what's your thoughts?

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:02.140
**Matt,** So this is interesting.

00:52:02.860 --> 00:52:04.700
No, I don't think it will,

00:52:04.700 --> 00:52:06.220
because it's like an overhyped word

00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:08.300
that means everything and nothing right now.

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:09.980
So there's no way anything

00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:11.900
could possibly live up to this hype, right?

00:52:11.900 --> 00:52:13.260
Unless by killing Selenium,

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:15.500
it kills half the planet and therefore,

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:18.140
some kind of doomsday terminator scenario.

00:52:18.140 --> 00:52:18.860
We're like, well, yeah,

00:52:18.860 --> 00:52:21.020
technically as an afterthought, right?

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:23.180
After effect, whatever.

00:52:24.140 --> 00:52:25.740
So a couple of years ago,

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:26.780
I had this idea,

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:28.940
and I guess I didn't think to put AI

00:52:28.940 --> 00:52:29.660
in the word of it

00:52:29.660 --> 00:52:31.740
because it wasn't this buzzword thing.

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:32.540
But I thought like,

00:52:32.540 --> 00:52:34.460
you know what definitely needs to stop happening

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:36.060
is people spending their whole day

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:38.220
staring at the Selenium API

00:52:38.220 --> 00:52:38.940
and trying to figure out

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:41.260
how to craft the perfect test.

00:52:41.340 --> 00:52:43.500
And I thought it would be more useful use of time

00:52:44.460 --> 00:52:47.660
to maybe look at Google analytical data,

00:52:47.660 --> 00:52:48.700
see where like basically

00:52:48.700 --> 00:52:50.300
that's that cow paths metaphor.

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:52.620
Find where people are going,

00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:54.220
which kind of means like

00:52:54.220 --> 00:52:55.340
put stuff out in production

00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:56.780
and see how people are using it

00:52:56.780 --> 00:52:59.580
and then use that to inform how you should test.

00:52:59.580 --> 00:53:01.420
But then take it one step further.

00:53:01.420 --> 00:53:03.660
If it now like if there's like this path

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:04.380
that's emerging

00:53:04.380 --> 00:53:06.300
because you've kind of instrumented things,

00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:08.140
why don't you just have that spit out

00:53:08.140 --> 00:53:10.700
the Selenium test suite for you, right?

00:53:10.780 --> 00:53:12.300
It's effectively converting a log file

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:13.260
to a Selenium test.

00:53:13.260 --> 00:53:14.700
And then taking it one step further,

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:16.140
not even like doing anything.

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:17.500
You could stop there and say,

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:19.260
okay, download your Selenium test suite

00:53:19.260 --> 00:53:20.940
and install it into your CI.

00:53:20.940 --> 00:53:23.100
But how about you go one step further beyond that

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:25.420
and it automatically runs

00:53:25.420 --> 00:53:27.180
basically today's production code

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:31.740
against your newest version

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:33.660
that's in source control,

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:35.260
but you haven't pushed to production yet, right?

00:53:35.260 --> 00:53:36.460
And so then you start doing

00:53:36.460 --> 00:53:38.620
kind of like an A-B test, if you will,

00:53:38.620 --> 00:53:41.100
of like, okay, you know, production,

00:53:41.100 --> 00:53:43.580
we had roughly this number of paths

00:53:43.580 --> 00:53:45.420
to sign up for credit cards and whatever,

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:47.340
run all effectively this log data

00:53:47.340 --> 00:53:49.660
against our production candidate.

00:53:49.660 --> 00:53:52.220
And as long as things aren't grossly out of bounds,

00:53:52.220 --> 00:53:56.620
then that's like it vaguely seems like we've tested things

00:53:56.620 --> 00:53:58.140
like we haven't broken anything

00:53:58.140 --> 00:53:59.980
and push that to production.

00:53:59.980 --> 00:54:01.180
And it basically get to a point

00:54:01.180 --> 00:54:03.660
where no one opened an IDE

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:07.260
and looked at the Selenium or the WebDriver API

00:54:07.260 --> 00:54:08.300
and wrote a test.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:09.660
Like the system wrote those tests.

00:54:10.220 --> 00:54:12.940
There is a patent out there.

00:54:12.940 --> 00:54:15.180
It's called, I thought it was rather clever,

00:54:15.180 --> 00:54:16.300
analytics-driven development.

00:54:16.300 --> 00:54:17.420
So I patented ADD.

00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:21.820
And it's this idea that you would take

00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:23.980
this analytical data

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:25.420
and you would generate test cases

00:54:25.420 --> 00:54:26.780
and then use those automatically

00:54:26.780 --> 00:54:28.380
to test your stuff that's out there.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:30.300
And I would argue that, well,

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:32.300
that's like a machine learning algorithm.

00:54:32.300 --> 00:54:33.580
That is AI.

00:54:33.580 --> 00:54:35.340
And so does that kill Selenium?

00:54:35.340 --> 00:54:36.540
No, like under the hood,

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:39.340
you would still have some automation engine

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:42.460
that would run steps that automate a browser.

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:43.900
So that doesn't go away.

00:54:43.900 --> 00:54:45.020
And if anything else, because-

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:46.540
But the human screwing it up does.

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:48.060
Yeah, maybe.

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:49.260
But you're going to screw things up

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:50.940
in awesome new ways.

00:54:52.300 --> 00:54:55.100
So it ends up being like all technology.

00:54:55.100 --> 00:54:58.060
It will now be pushed down

00:54:58.060 --> 00:54:59.900
into a lower level of the stack

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:02.140
that we're barely aware of.

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:04.060
And there's these metaphors

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:05.660
about leaky abstractions or something.

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:06.620
It'll be something like we're

00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:09.180
almost like compiling to assembly.

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:09.820
It's there.

00:55:09.820 --> 00:55:11.020
Someone super wants to geek out.

00:55:11.020 --> 00:55:11.980
They might have to pop the hood

00:55:11.980 --> 00:55:12.940
and go down there.

00:55:12.940 --> 00:55:14.220
But people will be spending

00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:15.580
all their time kind of manipulating

00:55:16.300 --> 00:55:17.660
machine learning kind of data sets

00:55:17.660 --> 00:55:19.340
of like, oh, how do we replicate

00:55:19.340 --> 00:55:19.980
production data

00:55:20.620 --> 00:55:21.660
before we push to production?

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:23.180
Like, oh, well, maybe find-

00:55:23.180 --> 00:55:24.380
There'll be all these arguments

00:55:24.380 --> 00:55:26.620
about how do we basically coach

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:30.060
again, from a cow paths metaphor,

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:32.610
how can we maybe herd

00:55:32.610 --> 00:55:33.570
these virtual animals,

00:55:33.570 --> 00:55:34.690
whatever you want to call it.

00:55:34.850 --> 00:55:36.050
How can we modify-

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:38.450
How do we create these data sets?

00:55:38.450 --> 00:55:40.290
How do we encourage this AI

00:55:40.290 --> 00:55:41.890
that is like almost like an-

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:44.610
Not so much an artificial intelligence

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:45.810
that can go back in time and kill us,

00:55:45.810 --> 00:55:47.490
but it might be like it's like a puppy

00:55:47.490 --> 00:55:48.690
or some sheep.

00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:51.330
Like, how do we modify these AI things

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:52.850
into kind of testing their software

00:55:52.850 --> 00:55:54.290
the way we want them to?

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:55.170
But under the hood,

00:55:55.170 --> 00:55:57.570
the engine that it will be doing

00:55:57.570 --> 00:55:58.530
to do its thing

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:00.370
will be Selenium or WebDriver, right?

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:02.130
But will people be writing Selenium tests?

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:03.570
Like, I'm not convinced

00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:04.690
that people will be,

00:56:04.690 --> 00:56:06.210
but they'll need to be able to read them

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:09.170
because if the AI spits out total garbage,

00:56:09.170 --> 00:56:10.370
someone will have to go in there

00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:10.930
and fix it.

00:56:10.930 --> 00:56:12.690
Just like today, someone somewhere

00:56:12.690 --> 00:56:14.610
has to go into some source code

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:15.890
and modify COBOL

00:56:15.890 --> 00:56:18.210
because like the IRS or Social Security

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:20.370
couldn't print checks last week.

00:56:20.370 --> 00:56:21.730
Like, that code's still there,

00:56:21.730 --> 00:56:22.850
but most of the time,

00:56:22.850 --> 00:56:24.050
you don't care about it.

00:56:24.050 --> 00:56:25.650
It'll hopefully just work.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:28.690
So maybe Selenium's future

00:56:28.690 --> 00:56:30.610
is that it's like COBOL.

00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:33.010
It's everywhere, but you don't care.

00:56:33.010 --> 00:56:34.050
Until you don't get paid.

00:56:34.610 --> 00:56:36.610
So I'm going to tweet that right now.

00:56:36.610 --> 00:56:39.810
Inventor of Selenium says it's just like COBOL.

00:56:41.890 --> 00:56:43.570
All right, thank you.

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:44.610
Yeah, yeah.

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:46.850
This has been awesome having you on.

00:56:46.850 --> 00:56:47.730
I love the stories.

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:49.810
I think it's good to hear that

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:51.650
it's good for you not to yell at us

00:56:52.370 --> 00:56:53.970
and find some agreement there

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:56.530
and point out our hyperbole.

00:56:56.530 --> 00:56:57.810
We appreciate that.

00:56:57.810 --> 00:56:59.730
But yeah, these stories are fantastic.

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:01.170
Thank you so much for volunteering

00:57:01.170 --> 00:57:02.370
to be on our show.

00:57:02.370 --> 00:57:02.690
Thanks.

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:03.810
All right.

00:57:04.450 --> 00:57:05.410
I'm still Alan.

00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:06.290
And I'm Brent.

00:57:06.290 --> 00:57:07.810
Oh, and I'm Jason Huggins.

00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:08.450
All right.

00:57:08.450 --> 00:57:09.090
Thanks, everybody.

00:57:09.090 --> 00:57:10.130
We'll see you next time.

00:57:10.130 --> 00:57:12.290
Bye.

